NON-EDGE-TO-EDGE HALF-POLYGONS — the arrangements Marek found missing      2026-08-17, CC
================================================================================================

MAREK CTRNACT, 2026-08-16 (Discord): "I think your half-polygons are missing some options. For
example arrangements like these" + a picture of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is divided in two.
AL: "they're not edge to edge". Marek: "One edge is simply divided in two."

He is right, and the picture understates it. The engine glues WHOLE edge to WHOLE edge, so every
board in the family misses the arrangements where one tile's edge is met by two or more tiles.

WHICH BOARDS CAN HOST ONE AT ALL. A tile's edge can be met by several neighbours only if its length
is a SUM of edge lengths the same palette offers. Measured over every Euclidean half-family palette
(scratch: splitcheck.mjs; lengths evaluated from the palette tokens, not eyeballed):

  eu-half-hex-v    1, 2                    2 = 1+1          SPLITTABLE   27,159 shipped
  eu-half-pent     1, 2, cot18=3.0777      2 = 1+1          SPLITTABLE      567 shipped
  eu-half-hex-m    1, 2, 2sqrt3=3.4641     2 = 1+1          SPLITTABLE        1 shipped
  eu-half-sq-mid   1, 2                    2 = 1+1          SPLITTABLE        1 shipped
  eu-half-oct-m    1, 2, 2+2sqrt2=4.8284   2 = 1+1          SPLITTABLE    RULED OUT
  eu-half-dec-v    1, 2phi=3.2361          none             safe          RULED OUT
  tri45all         S=1, H=sqrt2, D=2       D = S+S          SPLITTABLE    5,313 shipped
  tri45 (1 tile)   S=1, H=sqrt2            none             safe
  penrose          1, phi                  none             safe          4,505 shipped
  planigon         12 lengths              24 decompositions SPLITTABLE   1,055 shipped

  NOTE the correction to my own first reading: it is NOT only the cut edge that matters. pent, hex-m
  and oct-m all carry an ordinary side of length 2 alongside sides of length 1, so they split on a
  plain side even where the cut edge (cot18, 2sqrt3, 2+2sqrt2) decomposes into nothing.

  Marek's picture is the tri45 board: S=1, H=sqrt2, D=2, so the big triangle's hypotenuse D is
  exactly two unit legs. That board shipped in v1.24.0.

WHAT THIS DOES TO THE SIX-BOARD PROOF. Both filters behind it assume edge-to-edge, explicitly:
  - the angle filter needs a vertex's corners to sum to 360. On a divided edge the corners on one
    side sum to 180, so a*(alpha/2) + b*alpha = 360 is not the equation and (n-2)|8 does not follow.
  - the edge-slot filter says in its own words "each edge is shared by exactly TWO corners". A
    divided edge is shared by three. That is the argument that emptied n=10 vertex and n=8 midpoint.
  The DECAGON survives on the measurement above: no edge of it decomposes, so no divided edge is
  possible there and its parity argument still holds. The OCTAGON does not survive: its length-2
  side splits as 1+1, so "the alphabet has exactly 1 vertex configuration and the search returns 0"
  was computed under an assumption that no longer applies. It has to be re-run.

METHOD. One extra FLAT 180-degree corner at the division point turns the join back into an
edge-to-edge one the existing engine can search, which is AL's own proposal (2026-08-16: "change the
palette so that the triangle is a quadrilateral, with the hypotenuse made up of two halves"). The
machinery is already there: gen_alphabet's `doubled` and `scaled` kinds are built out of flat
corners, and eu_pruner groups by the Myers counting convention, which does not count a flat-flat
mid-edge vertex, so k stays comparable with the shipped numbers.

The split palette REPLACES the unsplit one rather than joining it: two tiles meeting along their
full long sides put their flat corners at the same point and 180+180 closes a legal vertex, so the
edge-to-edge tilings stay reachable. Expect counts >= the shipped ones; anything less is a bug.

BASELINE, the shipped counts (lib/tilings/eu-half.ts)
  hexv    k1 2   k2 4   k3 15   k4 34   k5 62   k6 166   k7 226   k8 752 ... k13 8418   (27,159)
  pent    k1 2   k2 1   k3 2    k4 3    k5 6    k6 7     k7 14 ... k14 177             (567)
  hexm    k2 1                                                                          (1)
  sqmid   k1 1                                                                          (1)

RUNS

--- RUN 1: eu-half-hex-v-split, k<=3 ------------------------------------------------------------
Palette: the trapezoid [1,2,2,1] sides 1,1,1,2 remodelled as [1,2,2,1,3] with five unit sides, the
flat 3 (=180 deg) sitting at the midpoint of the long side.

FIRST ATTEMPT WAS WRONG, and the way it was wrong is now a fixed bug in gen_alphabet.py.
  k=1: 0   k=2: 10   k=3: 56      <- k=1 EMPTY where the unsplit board has 2. Not a superset.
  Cause: min_len. A 2-corner vertex configuration (the noncounting flat-flat mid-edge junction where
  two tiles abut along a FULL edge) is only enumerated when min_len=2, and the gate read
      has_reflex_composite = any(kind=="composite" and any(a > D//2 ...))
  A flat corner is a == D//2 exactly, not >, so the gate said no. With min_len=3 the mid-edge point
  is not a legal vertex at all: every edge-to-edge tiling was dropped, and in the survivors every
  mid-edge point was counted as a real vertex, inflating k by a tier. Alphabet: 0 noncounting entries
  out of 67, which is the fingerprint.
  Fix: `a >= D // 2`, so a composite carrying a FLAT corner gets min_len=2 like the doubled/scaled
  tiles it is a longhand spelling of. Checked first: no palette written before today has a composite
  with an angle exactly D/2, so no pinned table moves.

AFTER THE FIX
  alphabet 51 configs, 72 entries (was 50/67; the 5 new ones are the flat-flat junctions)
  k=1 : 6      unsplit 2
  k=2 : 53     unsplit 4
  k=3 : 306    unsplit 15
  total 365    unsplit 21

CONTAINMENT, exact. Reused the shelf builder's congruence predicate (point group = 6 rotations plus a
reflection, lattice carried onto lattice unimodularly, every tile matched), with collinear vertices
dropped first so a split face is compared as the trapezoid it actually is. Oracle self-check 12/12.
  all 21 unsplit tilings ARE present in the split run   (0 missing)
  344 of the 365 are NEW
So the remodelled palette is a verified superset, and the non-edge-to-edge arrangements outnumber the
edge-to-edge ones better than 16 to 1 by k=3.

