A SECOND BUG, FOUND BY THE COUNTS NOT ADDING UP. hexv k=1 came out as 3 tilings where the
edge-to-edge run finds 2 and where NO tiling has a T-junction, so the extra one could not be new. It
was the same tiling on a DOUBLED CELL: 4 tiles instead of 2. The flat corners give the search more
ways to label one geometry and some of them close on a lattice coarser than the tiling's own, and the
congruence dedup cannot catch it — that predicate maps lattice onto lattice UNIMODULARLY, and a
supercell is by definition not unimodularly related to the cell inside it. `isPrimitive` in the
builder now drops them: any translation carrying the tiling to itself is a difference of two
same-shape face centroids, so testing those against the marker set decides it.
  dropped: sqmid 63,031 of 80,676   hexv 11,015 of 62,069   hexm 8 of 101   pent 0
  After it, hexv k=1 is 2 again and matches the edge-to-edge run exactly.

CERTIFICATES all pass on the rebuilt shelf: shape <= 1.1e-9, area <= 4.7e-10, worst vertex 360.00
degrees. The angle certificate is run WITH the flat corners still on, where a divided edge contributes
its 180 and every vertex still closes at exactly 360; the shipped faces have them dropped, so the
catalogue says quadrilateral and the renderer draws one.

NO REGRESSION: every (board, k) count is >= the 2026-08-14 value, checked pairwise.

STILL OPEN
  · The T-junction label reconciles exactly on pent, hexm, sqmid and on hexv k=1 and k=3, and reports
    9 more "edge-to-edge" than the original enumeration across hexv k=2 and k=4. Until that is run
    down the note claims a POSITIVE T-junction count and never claims the absence of one.
  · tri45all (Marek's own picture) is measured — 1309 preserved, 992 new at k<=3 — and NOT shipped.
    Its shelf is built by a different script and the same supercell filter has to go in first.
  · planigons have 24 decompositions across 12 edge lengths, several three-part and several with
    asymmetric split points, so that board needs a variant per decomposition, not one per tile.
  · The half-octagon re-ran empty to k=4, so the six-board family stands — but on a re-run, not on
    the parity argument, which does not apply once an edge can be divided.

--- RUN 7: the 30-60-90, the sixth board and the one with no board -------------------------------
AL asked directly (2026-08-17): what about the half-EQUILATERAL-TRIANGLE, hypotenuse divided in half?

It is the n=3 mirror cut, the sixth member of the family, and the only member the atlas has never
catalogued as a board: it ships as P12.6.4 inside the planigon palette, one tile among fifteen. Sides
1, sqrt3, 2, so the hypotenuse is exactly TWO SHORT LEGS — the most divisible edge in the family, and
the case Marek's picture shows most directly. New palettes eu-half-tri-mirror{,-split}, D=12,
angles [1,2,3] -> [1,6,2,3] with the flat 6 (=180 deg) at the hypotenuse midpoint.

  k       edge-to-edge      divisible hypotenuse
  1              4                   5
  2             28                  62
  3            104                 378
  4            364                1937
  TOTAL    ~500  (514 raw)     ~2382  (2472 raw, 23 supercells dropped)

  Containment: all 514 edge-to-edge records present in the split run, 0 missing, exact congruence.
  Roughly 4.8x, and it starts at k=1: 4 becomes 5, so even the vertex-transitive case gains one.

  k<=6 was attempted and abandoned: the alphabet goes from 15 configs to 253, and the k=6 solve was
  still running past 18,386 raw solutions after 15 minutes. The edge-to-edge board alone reaches k=6
  in seconds (4,837 raw), which is the same depth/breadth trade the other split boards show.

⚑ NOT SHELVED. This board is absent from the euhalf shelf by construction — that shelf is the four
boards whose halves have no other home, and the header says outright that the half-triangle lives in
the planigon palette. Shelving it means either a fifth euhalf board (and rewriting that sentence) or a
split planigon palette, which needs a variant per decomposition: 24 of them across 12 edge lengths.
Either is a real change and neither is a rebuild of what already ships. Numbers above stand on their
own and are reproducible from the two palettes.

--- SHELVED: the 30-60-90 becomes the fifth euhalf board ------------------------------------------
AL, 2026-08-17: "Add it to the atlas." Taken as the fifth euhalf board rather than a split planigon
palette — the planigon route needs a variant per decomposition (24 across 12 edge lengths) and would
rebuild a shelf that is not the subject. Id `tri`, following the `pent` precedent: an odd polygon has
one cut, so the board is named for the polygon and not the cut.

  tri  { 1:5  2:64  3:391  4:1989 | 5:1043  6:3280 }   6,772 tilings, dividedTo=4, enumeratedTo=6
  shelf 28,715 -> 35,487 over five boards
