THREE THINGS THE NEW BOARD BROKE, all in the guard test, all real.

  THE CUT IS NOT ALWAYS THE LONGEST SIDE. The test asserted `max(sides) === cut length`, which holds
  on every other board and fails here: the equilateral triangle's altitude is sqrt3 = 1.732 while the
  hypotenuse it leaves is the original edge, 2. Now it asserts the cut is PRESENT among the sides and
  checks the others against that index. The old form was true of four boards by coincidence.

  THE FAMILY LIST. "Exactly one live board is elsewhere (n=4 vertex, the tri45 shelf)" replaces "two
  of them are on the planigon and tri45 shelves", in the test, in lib/tilings/eu-half.ts and in the
  /library class note, which claimed the half triangle was one of the planigons. It is its own board.

  THE EAGER FILE CROSSED THE PACKING THRESHOLD. `stringifyAtlas` emits the container format
  ({atlas,dict,records}) once a file is big enough, and adding 2,449 k<=4 records tipped
  reference-atlas-euhalf.json over. The test read it with a bare JSON.parse and got an object:
  "rows.filter is not a function". decodeAtlas now, same as every other reader. Worth noting the
  failure mode is silent for anything that iterates instead of calling an array method.
